\subsection{Graceful Shutdown}

A well-behaved BitTorrent client must handle termination gracefully. When the
user presses Ctrl+C, we cannot simply abort---doing so would leave the tracker
unnotified (no [[event=stopped]] announce), potentially corrupt partial downloads,
and leave peer connections hanging. Instead, we intercept the SIGINT signal and
initiate a coordinated shutdown sequence.

\subsubsection{Shutdown Sequence Diagram}

The following diagram shows the interaction between components during shutdown:

\begin{verbatim}
  User          Signal Handler      Event Loop       Tracker         Peers
   |                  |                  |               |              |
   | Ctrl+C           |                  |               |              |
   |----------------->|                  |               |              |
   |                  | set shutdown     |               |              |
   |                  | flag (atomic)    |               |              |
   |                  |----------------->|               |              |
   |                  |                  |               |              |
   |                  |      detect flag |               |              |
   |                  |      in select!  |               |              |
   |                  |                  |               |              |
   |                  |                  | event=stopped |              |
   |                  |                  |-------------->|              |
   |                  |                  |               |              |
   |                  |                  |     (5s timeout, best-effort)|
   |                  |                  |<--------------|              |
   |                  |                  |               |              |
   |                  |                  | close connections            |
   |                  |                  |----------------------------->|
   |                  |                  |               |              |
   |                  |                  | return ShutdownRequested     |
   |                  |                  |               |              |
   | exit(0)          |                  |               |              |
   |<-----------------|------------------|               |              |
\end{verbatim}

If the user presses Ctrl+C a second time before shutdown completes:

\begin{verbatim}
  User          Signal Handler
   |                  |
   | Ctrl+C (2nd)     |
   |----------------->|
   |                  | count >= 2?
   |                  | yes -> exit(130)
   |                  |
   | IMMEDIATE EXIT   |
   |<-----------------|
\end{verbatim}

The shutdown sequence involves several steps:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Signal interception}: Catch Ctrl+C and set a shutdown flag rather
  than terminating immediately.

\item \textbf{Tracker notification}: Send an announce with [[event=stopped]] to
  inform the tracker we're leaving the swarm. This allows the tracker to
  immediately remove us from its peer list rather than waiting for our entry
  to expire.

\item \textbf{Peer disconnection}: Close all peer connections cleanly, sending
  any final messages required by the protocol.

\item \textbf{Disk flush}: Ensure any pending piece writes are flushed to disk
  to prevent data loss.

\item \textbf{Progress feedback}: Display a ``Shutting down...'' message so the
  user knows the client is responding to their interrupt.

\item \textbf{Force exit}: If the user is impatient and presses Ctrl+C a second
  time, we exit immediately with code 130 (the standard Unix exit code for
  SIGINT termination: 128 + signal number 2).
\end{enumerate}

The stopped announce uses a shorter timeout (5 seconds) than regular announces
(30 seconds) because the user is waiting. If the tracker is unreachable, we
proceed with shutdown anyway---the best-effort nature means we try to be a good
citizen but don't block indefinitely on an unresponsive tracker.

\subsubsection{The ShutdownController}

The [[ShutdownController]] struct manages shutdown state using atomic variables.
This allows the signal handler (which runs asynchronously) to communicate with
the main event loop without locks.

<<shutdown controller>>=
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicBool, AtomicU8, Ordering};
use std::sync::Arc;

/// Exit code for SIGINT termination (128 + 2).
///
/// This follows the Unix convention where processes terminated by a signal
/// exit with code 128 + signal_number. SIGINT is signal 2.
pub const SIGINT_EXIT_CODE: i32 = 130;

/// Coordinates graceful shutdown across async tasks.
///
/// The `ShutdownController` provides thread-safe signaling for shutdown
/// requests. It tracks both the shutdown request and the number of times
/// Ctrl+C has been pressed, enabling the "press twice to force exit" pattern.
///
/// # Thread Safety
///
/// All operations use atomic variables with appropriate ordering, making
/// this safe to share across threads and async tasks without locks.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```
/// use literate_bittorrent::client::ShutdownController;
/// use std::sync::Arc;
///
/// let controller = Arc::new(ShutdownController::new());
///
/// // Check if shutdown was requested
/// if controller.is_shutdown_requested() {
///     println!("Shutting down...");
/// }
///
/// // Request shutdown (called from signal handler)
/// controller.request_shutdown();
/// assert!(controller.is_shutdown_requested());
/// ```
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct ShutdownController {
    /// Flag indicating shutdown has been requested.
    shutdown_requested: AtomicBool,

    /// Count of Ctrl+C presses received.
    ///
    /// First press initiates graceful shutdown.
    /// Second press forces immediate exit.
    ctrl_c_count: AtomicU8,
}

impl Default for ShutdownController {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self::new()
    }
}

impl ShutdownController {
    /// Creates a new ShutdownController in the non-shutdown state.
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            shutdown_requested: AtomicBool::new(false),
            ctrl_c_count: AtomicU8::new(0),
        }
    }

    /// Returns true if shutdown has been requested.
    ///
    /// This method is designed to be called frequently from event loops
    /// without significant overhead.
    #[inline]
    pub fn is_shutdown_requested(&self) -> bool {
        self.shutdown_requested.load(Ordering::Acquire)
    }

    /// Requests a graceful shutdown.
    ///
    /// This sets the shutdown flag and increments the Ctrl+C counter.
    /// If this is the second (or later) Ctrl+C, it forces immediate exit.
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// `true` if this was the first shutdown request, `false` if shutdown
    /// was already requested.
    pub fn request_shutdown(&self) -> bool {
        let count = self.ctrl_c_count.fetch_add(1, Ordering::SeqCst);

        if count >= 1 {
            // Second Ctrl+C: force exit immediately
            eprintln!("\nForced exit.");
            std::process::exit(SIGINT_EXIT_CODE);
        }

        // First Ctrl+C: set shutdown flag
        let was_already_set = self.shutdown_requested.swap(true, Ordering::Release);
        !was_already_set
    }

    /// Returns the number of times Ctrl+C has been pressed.
    ///
    /// Useful for testing and debugging.
    pub fn ctrl_c_count(&self) -> u8 {
        self.ctrl_c_count.load(Ordering::Acquire)
    }

    /// Resets the controller to its initial state.
    ///
    /// This is primarily useful for testing.
    #[cfg(test)]
    pub fn reset(&self) {
        self.shutdown_requested.store(false, Ordering::Release);
        self.ctrl_c_count.store(0, Ordering::Release);
    }
}
@

The [[ShutdownController]] uses [[Ordering::Acquire]] and [[Ordering::Release]]
for the shutdown flag to ensure proper synchronization between the signal
handler and the main loop. For the Ctrl+C counter, we use [[Ordering::SeqCst]]
to ensure the increment and check happen atomically.

\subsubsection{Signal Handler Setup}

The [[install\_signal\_handler]] function sets up the Ctrl+C handler using
Tokio's signal API. It spawns a background task that waits for SIGINT signals
and calls the shutdown controller when received.

<<signal handler setup>>=
/// Installs a Ctrl+C signal handler that triggers graceful shutdown.
///
/// This function spawns a background task that listens for SIGINT (Ctrl+C)
/// signals. When received, it:
///
/// 1. Prints a "Shutting down..." message to stderr
/// 2. Calls `request_shutdown()` on the provided controller
/// 3. If pressed a second time, forces immediate exit with code 130
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `controller` - Shared reference to the shutdown controller
///
/// # Panics
///
/// Panics if called outside of a Tokio runtime context.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```no_run
/// use literate_bittorrent::client::{ShutdownController, install_signal_handler};
/// use std::sync::Arc;
///
/// #[tokio::main]
/// async fn main() {
///     let controller = Arc::new(ShutdownController::new());
///     install_signal_handler(controller.clone());
///
///     // Your main loop here, checking controller.is_shutdown_requested()
/// }
/// ```
pub fn install_signal_handler(controller: Arc<ShutdownController>) {
    tokio::spawn(async move {
        loop {
            // Wait for Ctrl+C signal
            match tokio::signal::ctrl_c().await {
                Ok(()) => {
                    // Print shutdown message before potentially exiting
                    eprintln!("\nShutting down...");

                    // Request shutdown (may force exit on second press)
                    controller.request_shutdown();
                }
                Err(e) => {
                    // Signal handling failed - log and continue
                    // This is rare but can happen on some platforms
                    eprintln!("Warning: Failed to listen for Ctrl+C: {}", e);
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
    });
}
@

The handler runs in a loop because after the first Ctrl+C, we want to continue
listening for a potential second press that would trigger force exit. The loop
terminates only if signal listening fails entirely, which is rare.

Note that we print the ``Shutting down...'' message \emph{before} calling
[[request\_shutdown]]. This ensures the user sees feedback even if the
subsequent shutdown logic takes time.

\subsubsection{Sending the Stopped Announce}

When our client shuts down gracefully, we must notify the tracker by sending an
announce with [[event=stopped]]. This is a matter of good swarm citizenship: it
allows the tracker to immediately remove us from its peer list, rather than
waiting for our entry to expire (typically after 30--60 minutes of inactivity).

Without this notification, other peers might waste connection attempts trying
to reach us, and the tracker's peer count would be temporarily inflated. While
the protocol can tolerate missing ``stopped'' events (clients crash, networks
fail), sending one when we can is the polite thing to do.

The shutdown announce has special requirements compared to regular announces:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Best effort}: We attempt the announce but don't block shutdown on
  its success. If the tracker is unreachable or slow, we proceed with shutdown
  anyway. The user pressed Ctrl+C because they want to stop; we shouldn't make
  them wait indefinitely.

\item \textbf{Short timeout}: We use a 5-second timeout rather than the normal
  30-second tracker timeout. This balances giving the tracker a chance to receive
  our notification against keeping shutdown responsive.

\item \textbf{Logging}: We log the attempt at INFO level so the user knows we're
  being a good citizen. If the announce fails, we log a warning but don't treat
  it as a fatal error.
\end{enumerate}

<<shutdown announce>>=
use tracing::{debug, info, warn};
use crate::tracker::{TrackerRequest, TrackerEvent, build_announce_url, TrackerError};

/// Timeout for shutdown announce, in seconds.
///
/// This is shorter than the normal 30-second tracker timeout because we don't
/// want to delay shutdown excessively. 5 seconds is enough for most trackers
/// to receive and acknowledge the stopped event.
pub const SHUTDOWN_ANNOUNCE_TIMEOUT_SECS: u64 = 5;

/// Send a "stopped" event to the tracker during graceful shutdown.
///
/// This function notifies the tracker that we are leaving the swarm. It uses
/// a short timeout (5 seconds) and is best-effort: failures are logged but do
/// not prevent shutdown from proceeding.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `metainfo` - The torrent metadata containing the tracker URL
/// * `peer_id` - Our 20-byte peer identifier
/// * `port` - The port we were listening on
/// * `uploaded` - Total bytes uploaded during this session
/// * `downloaded` - Total bytes downloaded during this session
///
/// # Returns
///
/// * `Ok(())` if the tracker acknowledged our stopped event
/// * `Err(TrackerError)` if the announce failed (logged as warning)
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```ignore
/// // During graceful shutdown sequence:
/// info!("Notifying tracker of shutdown...");
/// if let Err(e) = send_stopped_announce(
///     &metainfo,
///     &peer_id,
///     port,
///     uploaded,
///     downloaded,
/// ).await {
///     warn!("Failed to notify tracker: {}", e);
/// }
/// ```
///
/// # Logging
///
/// - INFO: "Sending stopped announce to tracker"
/// - INFO: "Tracker notified of shutdown" (on success)
/// - WARN: "Failed to send stopped announce: {error}" (on failure)
pub async fn send_stopped_announce(
    metainfo: &Metainfo,
    peer_id: &[u8; 20],
    port: u16,
    uploaded: u64,
    downloaded: u64,
) -> Result<(), TrackerError> {
    info!("Sending stopped announce to tracker");

    // Build the request with event=stopped
    // Note: 'left' is 0 since we're stopping, not because we completed
    let request = TrackerRequest {
        info_hash: metainfo.info_hash,
        peer_id: *peer_id,
        port,
        uploaded,
        downloaded,
        left: 0, // Not strictly accurate, but trackers don't care for stopped events
        event: Some(TrackerEvent::Stopped),
        compact: true,
    };

    // Build the URL
    let url = build_announce_url(&metainfo.announce, &request);

    // Send with short timeout
    let client = reqwest::Client::builder()
        .timeout(Duration::from_secs(SHUTDOWN_ANNOUNCE_TIMEOUT_SECS))
        .build()
        .map_err(TrackerError::TrackerHttpError)?;

    let response = match tokio::time::timeout(
        Duration::from_secs(SHUTDOWN_ANNOUNCE_TIMEOUT_SECS),
        client.get(&url).send(),
    ).await {
        Ok(Ok(resp)) => resp,
        Ok(Err(e)) => {
            warn!("Failed to send stopped announce: {}", e);
            return Err(TrackerError::TrackerHttpError(e));
        }
        Err(_) => {
            warn!("Stopped announce timed out after {} seconds", SHUTDOWN_ANNOUNCE_TIMEOUT_SECS);
            return Err(TrackerError::TrackerTimeout);
        }
    };

    // Check status - we don't really care about the response body for stopped
    if response.status().is_success() {
        info!("Tracker notified of shutdown");
        Ok(())
    } else {
        let status = response.status();
        warn!("Tracker returned error status {} for stopped announce", status);
        // Convert to an error, though we don't fail shutdown for this
        Err(TrackerError::TrackerInvalidResponse(
            format!("tracker returned HTTP {}", status)
        ))
    }
}

/// Perform a graceful shutdown sequence.
///
/// This function coordinates all shutdown activities in the proper order:
/// 1. Send stopped announce to tracker (best effort, 5 second timeout)
/// 2. Close peer connections (placeholder for future implementation)
/// 3. Flush pending disk writes (placeholder for future implementation)
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `metainfo` - The torrent metadata
/// * `peer_id` - Our peer identifier
/// * `port` - The port we were listening on
/// * `uploaded` - Total bytes uploaded
/// * `downloaded` - Total bytes downloaded
///
/// # Returns
///
/// Returns `Ok(0)` on successful shutdown, `Ok(non-zero)` if some cleanup
/// failed but shutdown should proceed anyway.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```ignore
/// // In the main event loop, when shutdown is detected:
/// if shutdown_controller.is_shutdown_requested() {
///     let exit_code = perform_graceful_shutdown(
///         &metainfo,
///         client.peer_id(),
///         client.port(),
///         stats.uploaded,
///         stats.downloaded,
///     ).await;
///     std::process::exit(exit_code);
/// }
/// ```
pub async fn perform_graceful_shutdown(
    metainfo: &Metainfo,
    peer_id: &[u8; 20],
    port: u16,
    uploaded: u64,
    downloaded: u64,
) -> i32 {
    info!("Performing graceful shutdown...");

    // Step 1: Notify tracker (best effort)
    let tracker_result = send_stopped_announce(
        metainfo,
        peer_id,
        port,
        uploaded,
        downloaded,
    ).await;

    if let Err(ref e) = tracker_result {
        // Already logged in send_stopped_announce, just note we're continuing
        warn!("Continuing shutdown despite tracker notification failure: {}", e);
    }

    // Step 2: Close peer connections
    // TODO: Implement when peer connection management is added
    // For now, just log that we would close connections
    info!("Closing peer connections...");

    // Step 3: Flush disk writes
    // TODO: Implement when disk I/O is fully integrated
    // For now, just log that we would flush
    info!("Flushing pending disk writes...");

    // Return success even if tracker notification failed
    // The important thing is we tried to be a good citizen
    info!("Shutdown complete");
    0
}
@

The [[send_stopped_announce]] function is intentionally simple: it builds the
request, sends it, and returns. Error handling is minimal because we don't want
complex retry logic to delay shutdown. The [[perform_graceful_shutdown]] wrapper
coordinates the full shutdown sequence and will be extended as we implement
more functionality.

Note that we set [[left: 0]] in the stopped request. Strictly speaking, this
should be the number of bytes remaining, but for a stopped event the tracker
doesn't use this value---it's removing us from the swarm regardless. Using 0
simplifies the function signature by not requiring the caller to pass the
current download state.

The function uses [[tokio::time::timeout]] in addition to the reqwest timeout
as a belt-and-suspenders approach. This ensures we never wait longer than
5 seconds, even if reqwest's timeout handling has edge cases.

