\subsection{Framed Message Reading}

After the handshake exchange completes, all subsequent peer communication uses
\emph{length-prefixed messages}. The BitTorrent protocol uses a 4-byte big-endian
length prefix that indicates how many bytes follow:

\begin{verbatim}
<length:4 bytes big-endian><message body:length bytes>
\end{verbatim}

This framing approach is common in binary protocols because it allows the receiver
to know exactly how many bytes to read before parsing. TCP is a stream protocol
with no inherent message boundaries; the length prefix provides that structure.

\subsubsection{Keep-Alive Messages}

A length of zero indicates a \emph{keep-alive} message. Keep-alives contain no
message ID and no payload---they are simply four zero bytes. Peers send keep-alives
every two minutes (per BEP 3) to prevent connection timeouts. Without keep-alives,
a connection that is idle for too long would be closed.

\subsubsection{Memory Exhaustion Protection}

A malicious or buggy peer could send a length prefix claiming a very large message
(up to 4GB, since the prefix is 32 bits). Naively allocating that much memory would
crash the client. We define a maximum message length and reject messages that exceed
it:

<<framed message reading>>=
use crate::messages::Message;

/// Maximum message size to prevent memory exhaustion.
///
/// BitTorrent messages are relatively small. The largest legitimate message
/// is a `Piece` message containing one block. Blocks are typically 16KB
/// (16384 bytes), and the message overhead is:
/// - 4 bytes: length prefix
/// - 1 byte: message ID
/// - 4 bytes: piece index
/// - 4 bytes: block offset
///
/// Total: 16393 bytes for a typical piece message.
///
/// We set the limit at 2^17 = 131072 bytes, which is 8x larger than a typical
/// piece message. This provides margin for:
/// - Slightly larger block sizes (some clients use 32KB blocks)
/// - Future protocol extensions
/// - Bitfield messages for very large torrents
///
/// Any message claiming to be larger than this is almost certainly:
/// - A bug in the peer's implementation
/// - A malicious attempt at memory exhaustion
/// - Corrupted data on the wire
pub const MAX_MESSAGE_LENGTH: u32 = 131072;

/// Reads one complete length-prefixed message from the stream.
///
/// The BitTorrent protocol uses a 4-byte big-endian length prefix:
/// - Length of 0 indicates a keep-alive message
/// - Otherwise, length bytes follow containing the message
///
/// This function handles all the complexities of reading from a TCP stream:
/// - Ensures we receive the complete length prefix (4 bytes)
/// - Validates the length against [`MAX_MESSAGE_LENGTH`]
/// - Reads exactly the right number of body bytes
/// - Handles clean disconnection vs errors
///
/// # Protocol Details
///
/// The message format after handshake is:
/// ```text
/// <length:4><id:1><payload:varies>
/// ```
///
/// Where length = 0 means keep-alive (no id, no payload), and length > 0
/// means a message with length = 1 + len(payload).
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `stream` - A mutable reference to the TCP connection
///
/// # Returns
///
/// * `Ok(Message)` - A complete, valid message was received
/// * `Err(PeerError::ConnectionClosed)` - The peer closed the connection
/// * `Err(PeerError::MessageTooLarge)` - Length exceeds `MAX_MESSAGE_LENGTH`
/// * `Err(PeerError::IoError)` - Other I/O error during read
/// * `Err(PeerError::InvalidMessage)` - Message body is malformed
/// * `Err(PeerError::UnexpectedMessageId)` - Unknown message type
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```no_run
/// use tokio::net::TcpStream;
/// use literate_bittorrent::client::read_message;
/// use literate_bittorrent::messages::Message;
///
/// async fn example(stream: &mut TcpStream) {
///     match read_message(stream).await {
///         Ok(Message::KeepAlive) => println!("Keep-alive received"),
///         Ok(Message::Choke) => println!("Peer choked us"),
///         Ok(Message::Unchoke) => println!("Peer unchoked us"),
///         Ok(msg) => println!("Received: {:?}", msg),
///         Err(e) => eprintln!("Error: {}", e),
///     }
/// }
/// ```
pub async fn read_message(stream: &mut TcpStream) -> Result<Message, PeerError> {
    // Read 4-byte length prefix
    let mut len_buf = [0u8; 4];
    match stream.read_exact(&mut len_buf).await {
        Ok(_) => {},
        Err(e) if e.kind() == std::io::ErrorKind::UnexpectedEof => {
            return Err(PeerError::ConnectionClosed);
        },
        Err(e) => return Err(PeerError::IoError(e)),
    }

    let len = u32::from_be_bytes(len_buf);

    // Keep-alive message (length = 0)
    if len == 0 {
        return Ok(Message::KeepAlive);
    }

    // Check for oversized messages
    if len > MAX_MESSAGE_LENGTH {
        return Err(PeerError::MessageTooLarge(len));
    }

    // Read message body
    let mut body = vec![0u8; len as usize];
    match stream.read_exact(&mut body).await {
        Ok(_) => {},
        Err(e) if e.kind() == std::io::ErrorKind::UnexpectedEof => {
            return Err(PeerError::ConnectionClosed);
        },
        Err(e) => return Err(PeerError::IoError(e)),
    }

    // Reconstruct the full message with length prefix for parsing.
    // Message::from_bytes expects the complete wire format: <length><id><payload>
    let mut full_message = Vec::with_capacity(4 + body.len());
    full_message.extend_from_slice(&len_buf);
    full_message.extend_from_slice(&body);

    // Parse message
    Message::from_bytes(&full_message)
}
@

The function follows a careful sequence:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Read length prefix}: We use [[read_exact]] to ensure we get all 4 bytes.
  If the peer closes the connection during this read (indicated by [[UnexpectedEof]]),
  we return [[ConnectionClosed]] rather than a generic I/O error.

\item \textbf{Handle keep-alive}: A zero length is a valid keep-alive message, not an
  error. We return immediately without trying to read a body.

\item \textbf{Validate length}: Before allocating memory for the body, we check against
  [[MAX_MESSAGE_LENGTH]]. This prevents memory exhaustion attacks.

\item \textbf{Read body}: With the length validated, we allocate exactly the right
  amount of memory and read the complete body.

\item \textbf{Parse message}: Finally, we reconstruct the complete message (with
  length prefix) and delegate to [[Message::from_bytes]] for parsing.
\end{enumerate}

Note that we reconstruct the full message with the length prefix before parsing.
This is because [[Message::from_bytes]] expects the complete wire format including
the length prefix. While this involves an extra copy, it keeps the parsing logic
centralized and consistent with how messages are serialized.

