\subsection{Piece Completion Cancellation}

When a piece is completed (all blocks received and hash verified), we must cancel
any pending requests for blocks of that piece from other peers. This is important
for two reasons:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Bandwidth conservation}: Receiving duplicate blocks wastes bandwidth.
  The peer is transmitting data we already have, and we cannot use that bandwidth
  for blocks we still need.

\item \textbf{Peer resources}: The remote peer allocates upload bandwidth and
  buffer space for our request. Cancelling promptly lets them serve other clients
  or our own future requests more efficiently.
\end{enumerate}

The completing peer (the one that sent the final block) is excluded from
cancellation---they have already fulfilled their request, so there is nothing
to cancel.

<<piece cancel handling>>=
/// Cancels all pending requests for a completed piece from all peers except
/// the one that completed it.
///
/// When a piece is verified as complete, any outstanding block requests for
/// that piece to other peers become unnecessary. This function:
///
/// 1. Iterates over all peer request queues
/// 2. Skips the completing peer (they already delivered)
/// 3. Removes all pending requests for the piece from each queue
/// 4. Returns `(peer_addr, Cancel)` pairs for the caller to send
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `piece_index` - The index of the completed piece
/// * `completing_peer` - Address of the peer that delivered the final block
/// * `request_queues` - Mutable map of all peer request queues
///
/// # Returns
///
/// A vector of `(SocketAddr, Message)` pairs. Each entry represents a cancel
/// message that should be sent to the peer at the given address. The caller
/// is responsible for actually sending these messages.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```ignore
/// // Piece 5 was just completed by peer at addr1
/// let cancels = cancel_piece_requests(5, addr1, &mut request_queues);
///
/// for (peer_addr, cancel_msg) in cancels {
///     send_message(peer_addr, cancel_msg).await;
/// }
/// ```
pub fn cancel_piece_requests(
    piece_index: u32,
    completing_peer: SocketAddr,
    request_queues: &mut HashMap<SocketAddr, PeerRequestQueue>,
) -> Vec<(SocketAddr, Message)> {
    let mut cancels = Vec::new();

    for (addr, queue) in request_queues.iter_mut() {
        // Remove all requests for this piece from this peer's queue
        let removed = queue.remove_for_piece(piece_index);

        // Skip generating Cancel messages for the completing peer---they already
        // delivered the piece, so there's nothing to cancel. We still clean up
        // their queue above to avoid stale state.
        if *addr == completing_peer {
            debug!(
                peer = %addr,
                piece_index,
                removed_count = removed.len(),
                "cleaned up completing peer queue (no cancel messages needed)"
            );
            continue;
        }

        debug!(
            peer = %addr,
            piece_index,
            cancel_count = removed.len(),
            "generating cancel messages for piece"
        );

        // Convert each removed request into a Cancel message
        for timestamped in removed {
            let cancel = Message::Cancel {
                index: timestamped.request.piece_index,
                begin: timestamped.request.offset,
                length: timestamped.request.length,
            };
            cancels.push((*addr, cancel));
        }
    }

    cancels
}
