\section{The Main Event Loop}

The event loop is the heart of the BitTorrent client. It multiplexes all async
operations using [[tokio::select!]], orchestrating peer messages, timers, and
shutdown signals into a coherent download process.

\subsection{Event Loop Design}

The event loop must handle several concurrent operations:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Peer Messages}: Read messages from all connected peers concurrently.
  When a message arrives, route it to the appropriate handler.

\item \textbf{Request Timeout}: Periodically check for timed-out block requests
  and reassign them to other peers.

\item \textbf{Re-Announce Timer}: Contact the tracker at the specified interval
  to refresh the peer list and report progress.

\item \textbf{Keep-Alive Timer}: Send keep-alive messages to peers that haven't
  received data recently, preventing connection timeouts.

\item \textbf{Shutdown Signal}: Check for graceful shutdown requests (Ctrl+C)
  and exit cleanly.
\end{enumerate}

The [[tokio::select!]] macro provides the mechanism to await multiple futures
concurrently, completing when any one of them is ready. This creates an event-driven
architecture where the loop responds to whichever event occurs first.

\subsubsection{Understanding Async/Await in the Event Loop}

Rust's async/await provides cooperative concurrency without threads. When a future
yields (e.g., waiting for network I/O), the runtime can execute other tasks. The
[[tokio::select!]] macro extends this to multiple futures, racing them against
each other:

\begin{verbatim}
tokio::select! {
    result = peer_read() => handle_peer_message(result),
    _ = timer.tick() => handle_timeout(),
    _ = shutdown_signal => break,
}
\end{verbatim}

This pattern is fundamentally different from threading. In a threaded model, each
peer would have its own thread, with synchronization primitives protecting shared
state. In our async model, there is \emph{one logical thread of execution}, with
futures taking turns. This eliminates data races by construction: when we access
[[PieceManager]], no other future is running.

The [[biased]] modifier in [[tokio::select!]] is crucial for our shutdown handling.
Without it, select! polls branches in random order. With [[biased]], branches are
checked in declaration order, giving shutdown detection highest priority:

\begin{verbatim}
tokio::select! {
    biased;  // Check branches in order

    // Branch 1: Highest priority - shutdown
    _ = ready(()), if shutdown_requested => Shutdown,

    // Branch 2: Timers
    _ = timeout_timer.tick() => RequestTimeoutCheck,

    // Branch 3: Peer messages (lower priority than timers)
    msg = read_peer() => PeerMessage(msg),
}
\end{verbatim}

When shutdown is requested, the first branch completes immediately via
[[std::future::ready]], and because of [[biased]], it is always selected even if
peer messages are also ready.

\subsubsection{Timer Management}

The event loop uses two timer strategies:

\textbf{Interval timers} (via [[tokio::time::interval]]) fire repeatedly at fixed
intervals. We use these for periodic tasks that should happen at consistent rates:
keep-alive checks, progress display, and choking reevaluation.

\textbf{One-shot sleeps} (via [[tokio::time::sleep]]) are used when the next interval
is dynamic. The tracker re-announce timer exemplifies this: on success, we use the
tracker-provided interval; on failure, we apply exponential backoff. After each
re-announce, we create a fresh [[Sleep]] future with the appropriate duration:

\begin{verbatim}
let mut re_announce_sleep = Box::pin(sleep(Duration::from_secs(interval)));

loop {
    tokio::select! {
        _ = &mut re_announce_sleep => {
            let next_interval = handle_re_announce().await;
            re_announce_sleep = Box::pin(sleep(next_interval));
        }
        // ... other branches
    }
}
\end{verbatim}

The [[Box::pin]] is necessary because [[Sleep]] implements [[Future]], and select!
requires pinned futures to poll them repeatedly.

\subsection{Event Types}

We define an enum to represent the different events the loop handles:

<<event loop>>=
use tokio::time::{interval, sleep, Sleep};
use std::pin::Pin;
use tracing::trace;

/// Request timeout check interval in seconds.
///
/// The event loop checks for timed-out requests every 30 seconds.
/// This is a balance between responsiveness (detecting timeouts quickly)
/// and overhead (not checking too frequently).
pub const REQUEST_TIMEOUT_CHECK_INTERVAL_SECS: u64 = 30;

/// Progress display interval in seconds.
///
/// The event loop displays download progress every 2 seconds. This provides
/// a good balance between keeping the user informed and not spamming output.
pub const PROGRESS_DISPLAY_INTERVAL_SECS: u64 = 2;

/// Choking reevaluation interval in seconds.
///
/// The event loop reevaluates choking decisions every 10 seconds. This ensures
/// newly-interested peers are promptly unchoked. Since we are a leecher-only
/// client, we use a simplified strategy: unchoke all interested peers to
/// encourage reciprocal unchoking.
pub const CHOKE_INTERVAL_SECS: u64 = 10;

/// Events that can occur during the event loop.
///
/// Each variant represents a distinct type of event that requires
/// handling in the main loop. The event loop uses `tokio::select!`
/// to await these events concurrently.
#[derive(Debug)]
pub enum EventLoopEvent {
    /// A message was received from a peer.
    ///
    /// Contains the peer's address and the message. The address is used
    /// to look up the connection state for message processing.
    PeerMessage {
        /// Socket address of the peer that sent the message.
        addr: SocketAddr,
        /// The received message or an error if parsing failed.
        message: Result<Message, PeerError>,
    },

    /// A peer connection was lost or errored.
    ///
    /// The connection should be removed and replaced from the peer pool.
    PeerDisconnected {
        /// Socket address of the disconnected peer.
        addr: SocketAddr,
        /// Human-readable description of why the connection was lost.
        reason: String,
    },

    /// Time to check for timed-out block requests.
    ///
    /// Triggered every REQUEST_TIMEOUT_CHECK_INTERVAL_SECS seconds.
    RequestTimeoutCheck,

    /// Time to re-announce to the tracker.
    ///
    /// Triggered at the interval specified by the tracker response.
    ReAnnounce,

    /// Time to send keep-alives to idle peers.
    ///
    /// Triggered every KEEP_ALIVE_INTERVAL_SECS seconds.
    KeepAliveCheck,

    /// Shutdown was requested (Ctrl+C or programmatic).
    Shutdown,

    /// Download is complete (all pieces verified).
    DownloadComplete,

    /// Time to display download progress.
    ///
    /// Triggered every PROGRESS_DISPLAY_INTERVAL_SECS seconds to show
    /// the user how the download is progressing.
    ProgressDisplay,

    /// Time to reevaluate choking decisions.
    ///
    /// Triggered every CHOKE_INTERVAL_SECS seconds. For our leecher-only
    /// client, this means unchoking all interested peers to encourage
    /// reciprocal unchoking.
    ChokingReevaluation,

    /// Time to check for stale peer connections.
    ///
    /// Triggered every STALE_PEER_CHECK_INTERVAL_SECS seconds. Stale peers
    /// are those that haven't provided useful data (blocks, bitfield, have
    /// messages) within PEER_STALE_TIMEOUT_SECS, even if they're sending
    /// keep-alives. These peers are disconnected to free connection slots
    /// for potentially more useful peers.
    StalePeerCheck,
}

/// Result of processing one event loop iteration.
///
/// Guides the main loop on what action to take next.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum EventLoopAction {
    /// Continue processing events.
    Continue,

    /// Exit the loop due to shutdown request.
    ShutdownRequested,

    /// Exit the loop because download is complete.
    Complete,

    /// Exit the loop due to an error.
    Error(String),
}
@

The event types capture the essential information needed to process each kind
of occurrence. The [[EventLoopAction]] enum tells the main loop whether to
continue or exit, and why.

\subsection{Event Loop Context}

The event loop needs access to various pieces of state. Rather than passing
many parameters, we group them into a context struct that can be passed around:

<<event loop>>=
/// Context for the event loop, holding references to all required state.
///
/// This struct groups together the state needed by the event loop, avoiding
/// long parameter lists and making the code more readable. The context
/// borrows state rather than owning it, so the event loop doesn't take
/// ownership of client resources.
pub struct EventLoopContext<'a> {
    /// Manages peer connections (add/remove/iterate).
    pub connection_manager: &'a mut PeerConnectionManager,

    /// Tracks piece download state and verification.
    pub piece_manager: &'a mut PieceManager,

    /// Tracks which pieces each peer has (for rarest-first).
    pub availability: &'a mut PieceAvailability,

    /// Signals for shutdown coordination.
    pub shutdown: &'a ShutdownController,

    /// Pending request queues per peer (keyed by address).
    ///
    /// Separated from PeerConnection to allow concurrent iteration.
    pub request_queues: &'a mut HashMap<SocketAddr, PeerRequestQueue>,

    /// In-progress piece buffers (keyed by piece index).
    ///
    /// Contains the partially-downloaded data for each piece.
    pub in_progress_pieces: &'a mut HashMap<u32, InProgressPiece>,

    /// Expected piece hashes for verification.
    pub piece_hashes: &'a [[u8; 20]],

    /// Number of pieces in this torrent.
    pub num_pieces: u32,

    /// Our peer ID for block requests.
    pub our_peer_id: [u8; 20],

    /// Tracker re-announce interval in seconds.
    pub tracker_interval: u64,

    /// Flag indicating download is complete.
    pub download_complete: &'a mut bool,

    /// Torrent metadata (for tracker URL).
    pub metainfo: &'a Metainfo,

    /// Info hash for tracker announces.
    pub info_hash: [u8; 20],

    /// Port we're listening on.
    pub port: u16,

    /// Consecutive re-announce failures (for backoff).
    pub re_announce_failures: &'a mut u32,

    /// Bytes verified at the last progress display.
    ///
    /// Used to calculate download speed as (current_bytes - last_bytes) / elapsed_time.
    pub last_progress_bytes: &'a mut u64,

    /// Instant when the last progress was displayed.
    ///
    /// Used together with `last_progress_bytes` to calculate speed.
    pub last_progress_time: &'a mut std::time::Instant,

    /// Instant when the download started.
    ///
    /// Used to calculate total elapsed time and average speed for completion message.
    pub download_start_time: std::time::Instant,

    /// Whether to emit JSON progress events instead of human-readable output.
    ///
    /// When enabled, progress updates are emitted as JSON objects to stderr,
    /// suitable for machine consumption by monitoring tools.
    pub json_log: bool,
}
@

\subsection{Reading from Peers}

Reading from peers in the event loop presents a challenge: we need to read
from any peer that has data available, but we can't hold mutable borrows of
all streams simultaneously. Instead, we poll peers round-robin within the
select! loop, reading from whichever has data available first.

Since tokio::select! already provides fair polling among its branches, and
peer reading is just one branch, we can use a simpler approach: try to read
from the first available peer connection.

<<event loop>>=
/// Reads a message from the first available peer connection.
///
/// This function tries to read from a single peer. The event loop calls
/// this repeatedly within select! to achieve fair multiplexing across
/// all ready events.
///
/// # Returns
///
/// - `Some((addr, result))` if we attempted to read from a peer
/// - `None` if there are no connections
async fn read_from_first_peer(
    connections: &mut HashMap<SocketAddr, PeerConnection>,
) -> Option<(SocketAddr, Result<Message, PeerError>)> {
    // Get first connection (HashMap iteration order is arbitrary but stable)
    let addr = *connections.keys().next()?;

    let conn = connections.get_mut(&addr)?;
    let result = read_message(&mut conn.stream).await;
    Some((addr, result))
}
@

\subsection{The Main Event Loop}

The main event loop ties everything together. It uses [[tokio::select!]] to
multiplex all event sources, processing whichever event occurs first.

<<event loop>>=
/// Runs the main event loop for the BitTorrent client.
///
/// This is the heart of the client, orchestrating all async operations:
/// - Reading messages from connected peers
/// - Checking for request timeouts
/// - Re-announcing to the tracker
/// - Sending keep-alive messages
/// - Handling graceful shutdown
///
/// The loop continues until:
/// - Shutdown is requested (returns `ShutdownRequested`)
/// - Download completes (returns `Complete`)
/// - An unrecoverable error occurs (returns `Error`)
///
/// # Event Priority
///
/// When multiple events are ready simultaneously, `tokio::select!` picks
/// one pseudo-randomly. This is fine for our use case since all events
/// need to be processed eventually.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `ctx` - Mutable reference to the event loop context containing all state
///
/// # Returns
///
/// `EventLoopAction` indicating why the loop exited.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```ignore
/// let action = run_event_loop(&mut ctx).await;
/// match action {
///     EventLoopAction::Complete => println!("Download finished!"),
///     EventLoopAction::ShutdownRequested => println!("Shutdown requested"),
///     EventLoopAction::Error(e) => eprintln!("Error: {}", e),
///     EventLoopAction::Continue => unreachable!("Loop should not return Continue"),
/// }
/// ```
pub async fn run_event_loop(ctx: &mut EventLoopContext<'_>) -> EventLoopAction {
    // Initialize timers
    let mut request_timeout_interval = interval(
        Duration::from_secs(REQUEST_TIMEOUT_CHECK_INTERVAL_SECS)
    );
    let mut keep_alive_interval = interval(
        Duration::from_secs(KEEP_ALIVE_INTERVAL_SECS)
    );
    let mut progress_display_interval = interval(
        Duration::from_secs(PROGRESS_DISPLAY_INTERVAL_SECS)
    );
    let mut choking_interval = interval(
        Duration::from_secs(CHOKE_INTERVAL_SECS)
    );
    let mut stale_peer_interval = interval(
        Duration::from_secs(STALE_PEER_CHECK_INTERVAL_SECS)
    );
    // Use sleep() for re-announce to support dynamic intervals (backoff on failure)
    let mut re_announce_sleep: Pin<Box<Sleep>> = Box::pin(
        sleep(Duration::from_secs(ctx.tracker_interval))
    );

    // Skip the first tick (intervals fire immediately on creation)
    request_timeout_interval.tick().await;
    keep_alive_interval.tick().await;
    progress_display_interval.tick().await;
    choking_interval.tick().await;
    stale_peer_interval.tick().await;
    // Note: re_announce_sleep doesn't need skipping - it's a one-shot sleep

    let mut iteration: u64 = 0;

    loop {
        iteration += 1;
        trace!(iteration, "event loop iteration start");

        // Check for shutdown first (non-blocking)
        if ctx.shutdown.is_shutdown_requested() {
            info!("shutdown requested, exiting event loop");
            return EventLoopAction::ShutdownRequested;
        }

        // Check for completion
        if *ctx.download_complete {
            info!("download complete, exiting event loop");
            return EventLoopAction::Complete;
        }

        // Wait for the next event
        let event = tokio::select! {
            biased;

            // Shutdown check has highest priority
            _ = std::future::ready(()), if ctx.shutdown.is_shutdown_requested() => {
                EventLoopEvent::Shutdown
            }

            // Request timeout check
            _ = request_timeout_interval.tick() => {
                EventLoopEvent::RequestTimeoutCheck
            }

            // Keep-alive check
            _ = keep_alive_interval.tick() => {
                EventLoopEvent::KeepAliveCheck
            }

            // Re-announce timer (uses sleep for dynamic intervals)
            _ = &mut re_announce_sleep => {
                EventLoopEvent::ReAnnounce
            }

            // Progress display timer
            _ = progress_display_interval.tick() => {
                EventLoopEvent::ProgressDisplay
            }

            // Choking reevaluation timer
            _ = choking_interval.tick() => {
                EventLoopEvent::ChokingReevaluation
            }

            // Stale peer check timer
            _ = stale_peer_interval.tick() => {
                EventLoopEvent::StalePeerCheck
            }

            // Read from peers (only if we have connections)
            Some((addr, result)) = read_from_first_peer_wrapper(
                ctx.connection_manager
            ) => {
                match result {
                    Ok(msg) => EventLoopEvent::PeerMessage { addr, message: Ok(msg) },
                    Err(PeerError::ConnectionClosed) => {
                        EventLoopEvent::PeerDisconnected {
                            addr,
                            reason: "connection closed".to_string(),
                        }
                    }
                    Err(e) => {
                        EventLoopEvent::PeerDisconnected {
                            addr,
                            reason: e.to_string(),
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        };

        trace!(?event, "processing event");

        // Handle re-announce specially to get the next interval
        if matches!(event, EventLoopEvent::ReAnnounce) {
            let next_interval = handle_re_announce(ctx).await;
            re_announce_sleep = Box::pin(sleep(next_interval));
            continue;
        }

        // Process all other events
        let action = handle_event(event, ctx).await;

        match action {
            EventLoopAction::Continue => continue,
            other => return other,
        }
    }
}

/// Wrapper to read from peers through the connection manager.
///
/// This function provides access to the internal connections HashMap
/// through the PeerConnectionManager interface.
async fn read_from_first_peer_wrapper(
    manager: &mut PeerConnectionManager,
) -> Option<(SocketAddr, Result<Message, PeerError>)> {
    // Get the connections as a mutable reference
    let connections = manager.connections_map_mut();
    read_from_first_peer(connections).await
}
@

The [[biased]] keyword in [[tokio::select!]] ensures that branches are checked
in order when multiple are ready. This gives shutdown the highest priority,
ensuring responsive termination.

\subsection{Event Handling}

Each event type has a corresponding handler that processes it and returns
an action for the main loop:

<<event loop>>=
/// Handles a single event from the event loop.
///
/// Dispatches to the appropriate handler based on event type and
/// returns an action indicating what the main loop should do next.
async fn handle_event(
    event: EventLoopEvent,
    ctx: &mut EventLoopContext<'_>,
) -> EventLoopAction {
    match event {
        EventLoopEvent::Shutdown => {
            info!("received shutdown event");
            EventLoopAction::ShutdownRequested
        }

        EventLoopEvent::DownloadComplete => {
            info!("download complete event");
            EventLoopAction::Complete
        }

        EventLoopEvent::PeerMessage { addr, message } => {
            handle_peer_message(addr, message, ctx).await
        }

        EventLoopEvent::PeerDisconnected { addr, reason } => {
            handle_peer_disconnected(addr, &reason, ctx).await
        }

        EventLoopEvent::RequestTimeoutCheck => {
            handle_request_timeouts(ctx).await
        }

        EventLoopEvent::KeepAliveCheck => {
            handle_keep_alives(ctx).await
        }

        EventLoopEvent::ProgressDisplay => {
            handle_progress_display(ctx);
            EventLoopAction::Continue
        }

        EventLoopEvent::ChokingReevaluation => {
            handle_choking_reevaluation(ctx).await
        }

        EventLoopEvent::StalePeerCheck => {
            handle_stale_peer_check(ctx).await
        }

        // ReAnnounce is handled specially in run_event_loop for dynamic intervals
        EventLoopEvent::ReAnnounce => {
            unreachable!("ReAnnounce events are handled in run_event_loop")
        }
    }
}
@

\subsubsection{Peer Message Handling}

When we receive a message from a peer, we route it to the appropriate handler
based on the message type:

<<event loop>>=
/// Handles an incoming message from a peer.
///
/// Routes the message to the appropriate handler and takes any
/// necessary follow-up actions (like requesting more blocks).
async fn handle_peer_message(
    addr: SocketAddr,
    message: Result<Message, PeerError>,
    ctx: &mut EventLoopContext<'_>,
) -> EventLoopAction {
    let msg = match message {
        Ok(m) => m,
        Err(e) => {
            debug!(peer = %addr, error = %e, "error receiving message");
            return handle_peer_disconnected(addr, &e.to_string(), ctx).await;
        }
    };

    // Get the connection (if it still exists)
    let conn = match ctx.connection_manager.get_connection_mut(&addr) {
        Some(c) => c,
        None => {
            debug!(peer = %addr, "received message from unknown peer");
            return EventLoopAction::Continue;
        }
    };

    // Update last receive time
    conn.mark_received();

    match msg {
        Message::KeepAlive => {
            trace!(peer = %addr, "received keep-alive");
        }

        Message::Choke => {
            let cancelled = handle_incoming_choke(conn);
            // Log cancelled requests - they will be available for re-request
            for req in cancelled {
                trace!(
                    piece = req.piece_index,
                    offset = req.offset,
                    "cancelled request returned to pool"
                );
            }
        }

        Message::Unchoke => {
            handle_incoming_unchoke(conn);
            conn.mark_useful_activity();  // Unchoke is useful - we can now request data
            // Trigger block requests now that we're unchoked
            trigger_block_requests_for_peer(addr, ctx).await;
        }

        Message::Interested => {
            conn.state.handle_interested();
            debug!(peer = %addr, "peer is interested in our pieces");
        }

        Message::NotInterested => {
            conn.state.handle_not_interested();
            debug!(peer = %addr, "peer is not interested in our pieces");
        }

        Message::Have(piece_index) => {
            conn.mark_useful_activity();  // Have is useful - tells us about piece availability
            let should_be_interested = handle_incoming_have(
                &mut conn.state,
                piece_index,
                ctx.availability,
                ctx.piece_manager,
            );

            if should_be_interested && !conn.state.am_interested {
                conn.state.am_interested = true;
                // Send Interested message
                if let Err(e) = write_message(&mut conn.stream, &Message::Interested).await {
                    debug!(peer = %addr, error = %e, "failed to send interested");
                } else {
                    conn.mark_sent();
                    debug!(peer = %addr, "sent interested after have");
                }
            }
        }

        Message::Bitfield(bits) => {
            conn.mark_useful_activity();  // Bitfield is useful - tells us what peer has
            match handle_incoming_bitfield(
                &mut conn.state,
                &bits,
                ctx.availability,
                ctx.piece_manager,
            ) {
                Ok(should_be_interested) => {
                    if should_be_interested && !conn.state.am_interested {
                        conn.state.am_interested = true;
                        if let Err(e) = write_message(&mut conn.stream, &Message::Interested).await {
                            debug!(peer = %addr, error = %e, "failed to send interested");
                        } else {
                            conn.mark_sent();
                            debug!(peer = %addr, "sent interested after bitfield");
                        }
                    }
                }
                Err(e) => {
                    // Invalid bitfield is a protocol error - keep at warn level
                    warn!(peer = %addr, error = %e, "invalid bitfield");
                    return handle_peer_disconnected(addr, &e.to_string(), ctx).await;
                }
            }
        }

        Message::Request { index, begin, length } => {
            // We don't serve requests (download-only client)
            debug!(
                peer = %addr,
                piece = index,
                offset = begin,
                len = length,
                "ignoring request (download-only client)"
            );
        }

        Message::Piece { index, begin, data } => {
            // Get or create in-progress piece
            let in_progress = ctx.in_progress_pieces.entry(index).or_insert_with(|| {
                let piece_size = ctx.piece_manager
                    .get_piece_size(index)
                    .unwrap_or(0);
                InProgressPiece::new(index, piece_size)
            });

            let expected_hash = ctx.piece_hashes.get(index as usize)
                .unwrap_or(&[0u8; 20]);

            // Need to get connection again due to borrow checker
            let conn = match ctx.connection_manager.get_connection_mut(&addr) {
                Some(c) => c,
                None => {
                    warn!(peer = %addr, "connection disappeared during piece handling");
                    return EventLoopAction::Continue;
                }
            };

            // Mark useful activity before processing - we received actual block data
            conn.mark_useful_activity();

            let result = handle_incoming_piece(
                conn,
                index,
                begin,
                &data,
                in_progress,
                expected_hash,
            );

            match result {
                PieceHandleResult::BlockStored => {
                    // Continue downloading - request more blocks if queue has space
                    trigger_block_requests_for_peer(addr, ctx).await;
                }
                PieceHandleResult::PieceVerified(piece_idx) => {
                    info!(piece = piece_idx, "piece verified!");

                    // Mark piece as verified in piece manager
                    if let Err(e) = ctx.piece_manager.mark_verified(piece_idx as u32) {
                        warn!(piece = piece_idx, error = %e, "failed to mark piece verified");
                    }

                    // Remove from in-progress
                    ctx.in_progress_pieces.remove(&(piece_idx as u32));

                    // Check if download is complete
                    if ctx.piece_manager.is_complete() {
                        *ctx.download_complete = true;
                        return EventLoopAction::Complete;
                    }

                    // Trigger requests for next pieces
                    trigger_block_requests_all_peers(ctx).await;
                }
                PieceHandleResult::PieceHashFailed(piece_idx) => {
                    warn!(piece = piece_idx, "piece hash verification failed, will retry");

                    // Mark the piece as failed so it can be re-downloaded
                    ctx.in_progress_pieces.remove(&(piece_idx as u32));
                    if let Err(e) = ctx.piece_manager.mark_failed(piece_idx as u32) {
                        warn!(piece = piece_idx, error = %e, "failed to mark piece as failed");
                    }

                    // Trigger requests to try again
                    trigger_block_requests_all_peers(ctx).await;
                }
                PieceHandleResult::UnexpectedBlock => {
                    // Ignore, already logged in handler
                }
            }
        }

        Message::Cancel { index, begin, length } => {
            // We don't serve requests, so we don't track cancellations
            trace!(
                peer = %addr,
                piece = index,
                offset = begin,
                len = length,
                "received cancel (ignored - download-only)"
            );
        }
    }

    EventLoopAction::Continue
}
@

\subsubsection{Peer Disconnection}

When a peer disconnects, we remove them from the connection pool and try to
replace them from the available peer queue:

<<event loop>>=
/// Handles peer disconnection.
///
/// When a peer disconnects, we must:
/// 1. Remove the peer from active connections
/// 2. Update piece availability (peer no longer has those pieces)
/// 3. Drain pending requests and attempt to reassign them to other peers
/// 4. Signal need for replacement peers if possible
/// 5. Handle the all-peers-disconnected case
///
/// # Request Reassignment
///
/// Pending block requests from the disconnected peer are not lost---we drain
/// them from the queue and attempt to reassign each to an alternative peer
/// that has the piece and is not choking us. If no alternative is available,
/// the request is effectively lost, but the piece will be re-requested during
/// normal download scheduling.
///
/// # All Peers Disconnected
///
/// When all peers disconnect, we log a warning and continue. The re-announce
/// timer will fire and fetch fresh peers from the tracker. Downloads cannot
/// proceed until new peers connect, but the event loop remains responsive.
async fn handle_peer_disconnected(
    addr: SocketAddr,
    reason: &str,
    ctx: &mut EventLoopContext<'_>,
) -> EventLoopAction {
    info!(peer = %addr, reason = %reason, "peer disconnected");

    // Remove from connection manager and update availability
    if let Some(conn) = ctx.connection_manager.remove_connection(&addr) {
        // Update availability - remove peer's pieces from global tracking
        ctx.availability.remove_peer_bitfield(&conn.state.bitfield);
    }

    // Drain pending requests before removing the queue
    // We attempt to reassign each request to an alternative peer
    let pending_requests: Vec<BlockRequest> = ctx.request_queues
        .remove(&addr)
        .map(|mut queue| queue.drain().map(|tr| tr.request).collect())
        .unwrap_or_default();

    let pending_count = pending_requests.len();
    let mut reassigned_count = 0;

    for request in pending_requests {
        // Find an alternative peer that has this piece and isn't choking us
        let alt_addr = find_alternative_peer(
            request.piece_index,
            addr,
            ctx.connection_manager.connections_map(),
        );

        if let Some(alt_addr) = alt_addr {
            debug!(
                piece = request.piece_index,
                offset = request.offset,
                old_peer = %addr,
                new_peer = %alt_addr,
                "reassigning pending request after disconnect"
            );

            // Queue the request to the alternative peer
            let alt_queue = ctx.request_queues.entry(alt_addr).or_insert_with(|| {
                PeerRequestQueue::new(DEFAULT_MAX_PIPELINE_LENGTH)
            });

            let new_request = BlockRequest::new(
                request.piece_index,
                request.offset,
                request.length,
                ctx.our_peer_id,
            );

            if alt_queue.push(new_request.clone()) {
                // Send the request message
                if let Some(alt_conn) = ctx.connection_manager.get_connection_mut(&alt_addr) {
                    let msg = Message::Request {
                        index: new_request.piece_index,
                        begin: new_request.offset,
                        length: new_request.length,
                    };
                    if let Err(e) = write_message(&mut alt_conn.stream, &msg).await {
                        debug!(peer = %alt_addr, error = %e, "failed to send reassigned request");
                        // Remove from queue since we couldn't send
                        alt_queue.remove(new_request.piece_index, new_request.offset);
                    } else {
                        alt_conn.mark_sent();
                        reassigned_count += 1;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        // If no alternative found, the request is dropped but will be re-requested
        // during normal scheduling when we have available peers
    }

    if pending_count > 0 {
        debug!(
            pending = pending_count,
            reassigned = reassigned_count,
            dropped = pending_count - reassigned_count,
            "completed request cleanup after disconnect"
        );
    }

    // Check connection status and log appropriately
    let active_count = ctx.connection_manager.active_count();
    let available_count = ctx.connection_manager.available_count();

    if active_count == 0 {
        // All peers disconnected - download stalls until re-announce
        warn!(
            available = available_count,
            "all peers disconnected, waiting for re-announce to get new peers"
        );
    } else if ctx.connection_manager.needs_more_connections() {
        debug!(
            active = active_count,
            available = available_count,
            "peer disconnected, replacement available in pool"
        );
    }

    EventLoopAction::Continue
}
@

\subsubsection{Request Timeout Handling}

Periodically, we check for timed-out block requests and reassign them:

<<event loop>>=
/// Handles the request timeout check timer.
///
/// Iterates through all peer request queues, finds timed-out requests,
/// and attempts to reassign them to other peers.
async fn handle_request_timeouts(
    ctx: &mut EventLoopContext<'_>,
) -> EventLoopAction {
    trace!("checking for request timeouts");

    // First pass: collect all timed-out requests with their peer addresses
    // This avoids borrowing issues by not holding mutable references across iterations
    let mut timed_out_requests: Vec<(SocketAddr, BlockRequest)> = Vec::new();

    let peer_addrs: Vec<SocketAddr> = ctx.request_queues.keys().copied().collect();

    for addr in peer_addrs {
        if let Some(queue) = ctx.request_queues.get(&addr) {
            for timestamped in queue.check_timeouts() {
                timed_out_requests.push((addr, timestamped.request.clone()));
            }
        }
    }

    // Second pass: process each timed-out request
    for (addr, request) in timed_out_requests {
        debug!(
            peer = %addr,
            piece = request.piece_index,
            offset = request.offset,
            "request timed out"
        );

        // Remove from the original peer's queue
        if let Some(queue) = ctx.request_queues.get_mut(&addr) {
            queue.remove(request.piece_index, request.offset);
        }

        // Create cancel message
        let cancel = Message::Cancel {
            index: request.piece_index,
            begin: request.offset,
            length: request.length,
        };

        // Send cancel to original peer
        if let Some(conn) = ctx.connection_manager.get_connection_mut(&addr) {
            if let Err(e) = write_message(&mut conn.stream, &cancel).await {
                debug!(peer = %addr, error = %e, "failed to send cancel");
            } else {
                conn.mark_sent();
            }
        }

        // Find an alternative peer that has the piece and isn't choking us
        let alt_addr = ctx.connection_manager
            .connections()
            .filter(|(peer_addr, _)| **peer_addr != addr)
            .filter(|(_, conn)| conn.state.has_piece(request.piece_index))
            .filter(|(_, conn)| !conn.state.peer_choking)
            .map(|(peer_addr, _)| *peer_addr)
            .next();

        // Re-request from alternative if available
        if let Some(alt_addr) = alt_addr {
            debug!(
                piece = request.piece_index,
                offset = request.offset,
                new_peer = %alt_addr,
                "reassigning timed-out request"
            );

            // Queue the request to the alternative peer
            if let Some(alt_queue) = ctx.request_queues.get_mut(&alt_addr) {
                let new_request = BlockRequest::new(
                    request.piece_index,
                    request.offset,
                    request.length,
                    ctx.our_peer_id,
                );
                alt_queue.push(new_request.clone());

                // Send the request message
                if let Some(alt_conn) = ctx.connection_manager.get_connection_mut(&alt_addr) {
                    let msg = Message::Request {
                        index: new_request.piece_index,
                        begin: new_request.offset,
                        length: new_request.length,
                    };
                    if let Err(e) = write_message(&mut alt_conn.stream, &msg).await {
                        debug!(peer = %alt_addr, error = %e, "failed to send request");
                    } else {
                        alt_conn.mark_sent();
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    EventLoopAction::Continue
}
@

\subsubsection{Keep-Alive Handling}

Send keep-alives to peers that haven't received data recently:

<<event loop>>=
/// Handles the keep-alive check timer.
///
/// Performs two functions:
/// 1. Disconnects peers that haven't sent any message in PEER_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECS
/// 2. Sends keep-alives to peers that haven't received data in KEEP_ALIVE_INTERVAL_SECS
async fn handle_keep_alives(
    ctx: &mut EventLoopContext<'_>,
) -> EventLoopAction {
    trace!("checking for keep-alives and idle peers");

    let idle_timeout = Duration::from_secs(PEER_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECS);
    let keep_alive_threshold = Duration::from_secs(KEEP_ALIVE_INTERVAL_SECS);

    // Collect addresses to avoid borrowing issues
    let peer_addrs: Vec<SocketAddr> = ctx.connection_manager
        .connections()
        .map(|(addr, _)| *addr)
        .collect();

    // First pass: identify and disconnect idle peers
    let mut idle_peers: Vec<SocketAddr> = Vec::new();
    for &addr in &peer_addrs {
        if let Some(conn) = ctx.connection_manager.get_connection(&addr) {
            if conn.last_recv_time.elapsed() >= idle_timeout {
                idle_peers.push(addr);
            }
        }
    }

    // Disconnect idle peers
    for addr in idle_peers {
        info!(peer = %addr, "disconnecting idle peer (no messages in {} seconds)", PEER_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECS);
        handle_peer_disconnected(addr, "idle timeout", ctx).await;
    }

    // Second pass: send keep-alives to remaining peers that need them
    // Re-collect addresses since some peers may have been disconnected
    let remaining_addrs: Vec<SocketAddr> = ctx.connection_manager
        .connections()
        .map(|(addr, _)| *addr)
        .collect();

    for addr in remaining_addrs {
        if let Some(conn) = ctx.connection_manager.get_connection_mut(&addr) {
            if conn.last_send_time.elapsed() >= keep_alive_threshold {
                match write_message(&mut conn.stream, &Message::KeepAlive).await {
                    Ok(()) => {
                        conn.mark_sent();
                        trace!(peer = %addr, "sent keep-alive");
                    }
                    Err(e) => {
                        debug!(peer = %addr, error = %e, "failed to send keep-alive");
                        // Don't disconnect here - let the next read fail
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    EventLoopAction::Continue
}
@

\subsubsection{Stale Peer Detection}

While the keep-alive handler disconnects peers that haven't sent \emph{any} message
(including keep-alives) within [[PEER\_IDLE\_TIMEOUT\_SECS]], some peers may keep
connections alive with keep-alives but never provide useful data. These ``stale''
peers waste connection slots that could be used by more productive peers.

The stale peer check identifies peers that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Are still connected and responding to keep-alives
\item Haven't provided useful data (blocks, bitfield, have messages) recently
\end{enumerate}

By disconnecting stale peers, we free up slots for new connections that might
be more productive.

<<event loop>>=
/// Handles the stale peer check timer.
///
/// Identifies and disconnects peers that haven't provided useful data
/// within PEER_STALE_TIMEOUT_SECS, even if they're still responding to
/// keep-alives. This frees connection slots for potentially more useful peers.
///
/// "Useful" data includes:
/// - Piece (block) messages: actual download data
/// - Bitfield messages: tells us what pieces the peer has
/// - Have messages: updates about piece availability
/// - Unchoke messages: allows us to request data
///
/// A peer sending only keep-alives or choke/interested messages is considered
/// stale because it's not contributing to the download.
async fn handle_stale_peer_check(
    ctx: &mut EventLoopContext<'_>,
) -> EventLoopAction {
    trace!("checking for stale peers");

    let stale_timeout = Duration::from_secs(PEER_STALE_TIMEOUT_SECS);

    // Collect addresses to avoid borrowing issues
    let peer_addrs: Vec<SocketAddr> = ctx.connection_manager
        .connections()
        .map(|(addr, _)| *addr)
        .collect();

    // Identify stale peers
    let mut stale_peers: Vec<SocketAddr> = Vec::new();
    for &addr in &peer_addrs {
        if let Some(conn) = ctx.connection_manager.get_connection(&addr) {
            if conn.last_useful_activity.elapsed() >= stale_timeout {
                stale_peers.push(addr);
            }
        }
    }

    // Disconnect stale peers
    for addr in stale_peers {
        info!(
            peer = %addr,
            timeout_secs = PEER_STALE_TIMEOUT_SECS,
            "disconnecting stale peer (no useful activity)"
        );
        handle_peer_disconnected(addr, "stale peer - no useful activity", ctx).await;
    }

    EventLoopAction::Continue
}
@

\subsubsection{Choking Reevaluation}

BitTorrent uses a tit-for-tat algorithm to encourage reciprocal sharing. The
classic algorithm works as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Regular unchokes}: Every 10 seconds, select the 4 peers with the
  highest upload rate to us (for leechers) or download rate from us (for seeders).
  Unchoke these peers; choke all others.

\item \textbf{Optimistic unchoke}: Every 30 seconds, randomly select one
  additional peer to unchoke. This allows new peers to prove themselves and
  prevents the system from getting stuck with suboptimal peer choices.
\end{enumerate}

However, since we are a leecher-only client (per PRD non-goals) that does not
upload data, we cannot use upload rates to rank peers. Instead, we use a
\emph{simplified choking strategy}:

\textbf{Simplified Strategy: Unchoke All Interested Peers}

Our algorithm is deliberately simple:

\begin{verbatim}
every 10 seconds:
    for each connected peer:
        if peer.interested AND we.am_choking:
            send Unchoke
            we.am_choking := false
        else if NOT peer.interested AND NOT we.am_choking:
            send Choke  (optional optimization)
            we.am_choking := true
\end{verbatim}

This strategy has several benefits for a download-only client:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Encourages reciprocity}: When we unchoke a peer, they may
  reciprocate by unchoking us. Even though we don't upload, being ``generous''
  signals good behavior.

\item \textbf{Simple implementation}: No need to track upload rates or maintain
  rolling averages. The logic is a simple conditional.

\item \textbf{Low overhead}: We only need to send Unchoke/Choke messages, not
  maintain complex data structures.
\end{enumerate}

The algorithm runs periodically (every [[CHOKE\_INTERVAL\_SECS]] = 10 seconds)
rather than immediately on Interested messages. This batches decisions and
keeps the logic centralized in one place.

<<event loop>>=
/// Handles the choking reevaluation timer event.
///
/// Implements simplified choking for a leecher-only client: unchoke all
/// interested peers. This encourages reciprocal unchoking without the
/// complexity of tracking upload rates or optimistic unchoking.
///
/// # Algorithm
///
/// For each connected peer:
/// 1. If they are interested AND we are currently choking them, send Unchoke
/// 2. Optionally, if they are not interested AND we are not choking them, send Choke
///
/// The second case is an optimization to free up resources when peers lose
/// interest, but is not strictly necessary for protocol correctness.
///
/// # Returns
///
/// Always returns `EventLoopAction::Continue` since choking decisions don't
/// affect the event loop's termination.
async fn handle_choking_reevaluation(
    ctx: &mut EventLoopContext<'_>,
) -> EventLoopAction {
    trace!("reevaluating choking decisions");

    // Collect addresses to avoid borrowing issues
    let peer_addrs: Vec<SocketAddr> = ctx.connection_manager
        .connections()
        .map(|(addr, _)| *addr)
        .collect();

    for addr in peer_addrs {
        if let Some(conn) = ctx.connection_manager.get_connection_mut(&addr) {
            // Unchoke interested peers that we are currently choking
            if conn.state.peer_interested && conn.state.am_choking {
                match write_message(&mut conn.stream, &Message::Unchoke).await {
                    Ok(()) => {
                        conn.state.am_choking = false;
                        conn.mark_sent();
                        debug!(peer = %addr, "unchoked interested peer");
                    }
                    Err(e) => {
                        debug!(peer = %addr, error = %e, "failed to send unchoke");
                        // Don't disconnect here - let the next read fail
                    }
                }
            }
            // Optional optimization: choke peers that are no longer interested
            else if !conn.state.peer_interested && !conn.state.am_choking {
                match write_message(&mut conn.stream, &Message::Choke).await {
                    Ok(()) => {
                        conn.state.am_choking = true;
                        conn.mark_sent();
                        debug!(peer = %addr, "choked uninterested peer");
                    }
                    Err(e) => {
                        debug!(peer = %addr, error = %e, "failed to send choke");
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    EventLoopAction::Continue
}
