\subsubsection{Re-Announce Handling}

When the re-announce timer fires, we contact the tracker again to update our
statistics and potentially receive new peers. The tracker response includes
an updated interval which we respect for the next re-announce.

The implementation handles tracker failures gracefully with exponential backoff:
on failure, we wait 30 seconds before the first retry, then double the wait time
for each subsequent failure (30s, 60s, 120s, 240s, 480s) capped at 15 minutes.
Successful re-announces reset the backoff. This prevents hammering a temporarily
unavailable tracker while ensuring we recover quickly when it returns.

The event loop uses [[sleep()]] rather than [[interval()]] for re-announce timing,
allowing dynamic adjustment of the next re-announce interval. On success, the
interval from the tracker response is used. On failure, a fixed base interval of
30 seconds is multiplied by the exponential backoff factor, capped at 15 minutes.

<<event loop>>=
use crate::tracker::announce;

/// Base backoff interval for tracker re-announce failures (30 seconds).
///
/// When a re-announce fails, we start with this interval and double it
/// for each consecutive failure (30s, 60s, 120s, 240s, 480s, ...).
const TRACKER_BACKOFF_BASE_SECS: u64 = 30;

/// Maximum backoff interval for tracker re-announce failures (15 minutes).
///
/// The exponential backoff is capped at this value to ensure we eventually
/// retry even after many consecutive failures.
const TRACKER_BACKOFF_MAX_SECS: u64 = 900;

/// Maximum consecutive announce failures before capping backoff.
///
/// After this many consecutive failures, we stop increasing the wait time.
/// With base interval of 30 seconds and 6 failures, the interval reaches
/// 30 * 2^5 = 960 seconds, which is capped at 900 seconds (15 minutes).
const MAX_CONSECUTIVE_ANNOUNCE_FAILURES: u32 = 6;

/// Handles the re-announce timer event.
///
/// Contacts the tracker with updated statistics (downloaded/uploaded/left),
/// merges any new peers into the available pool, and logs the result.
///
/// On tracker failure, the function logs at WARN level and continues.
/// The download can proceed with existing peers while waiting for the
/// tracker to recover.
///
/// # Backoff Strategy
///
/// - Success: Reset failure count, use tracker-provided interval
/// - Failure: Increment failure count, use exponential backoff with fixed base
///   - First failure: 30s
///   - Second failure: 60s
///   - Third failure: 120s
///   - And so on, capped at 15 minutes (900s)
///
/// # Returns
///
/// The duration to wait before the next re-announce. On success, this is
/// the interval from the tracker response. On failure, this is a fixed base
/// interval (30s) multiplied by an exponential backoff factor, capped at 15 minutes.
async fn handle_re_announce(
    ctx: &mut EventLoopContext<'_>,
) -> Duration {
    info!("re-announce timer fired, contacting tracker");

    // Build the request with current statistics
    let bytes_verified = ctx.piece_manager.bytes_verified();
    let total_length = ctx.piece_manager.total_length();
    let left = total_length.saturating_sub(bytes_verified);

    let request = TrackerRequest {
        info_hash: ctx.info_hash,
        peer_id: ctx.our_peer_id,
        port: ctx.port,
        uploaded: 0, // Download-only client
        downloaded: bytes_verified,
        left,
        event: None, // Regular re-announce, no special event
        compact: true,
    };

    // Perform the announce
    match announce(ctx.metainfo, request).await {
        Ok(result) => {
            // Reset failure count on success
            *ctx.re_announce_failures = 0;

            // Add peers to pool (announce() already parsed them)
            let peers_received = result.peers.len();
            let added = ctx.connection_manager.add_peers(result.peers);

            let next_interval = Duration::from_secs(result.interval);

            info!(
                peers_received = peers_received,
                peers_added = added,
                interval_secs = result.interval,
                seeders = ?result.seeders,
                leechers = ?result.leechers,
                downloaded = bytes_verified,
                left = left,
                "re-announce successful"
            );

            next_interval
        }
        Err(e) => {
            // Increment failure count (capped at max)
            *ctx.re_announce_failures = (*ctx.re_announce_failures + 1)
                .min(MAX_CONSECUTIVE_ANNOUNCE_FAILURES);

            // Calculate backoff: base * 2^(failures-1), capped at max
            // failures=1 -> 30s, failures=2 -> 60s, failures=3 -> 120s, etc.
            let backoff_multiplier = 1u64 << (*ctx.re_announce_failures - 1);
            let backoff_secs = TRACKER_BACKOFF_BASE_SECS
                .saturating_mul(backoff_multiplier)
                .min(TRACKER_BACKOFF_MAX_SECS);
            let next_interval = Duration::from_secs(backoff_secs);

            warn!(
                error = %e,
                consecutive_failures = *ctx.re_announce_failures,
                next_interval_secs = backoff_secs,
                "re-announce failed, continuing with existing peers"
            );

            next_interval
        }
    }
}
