\subsection{Error Handling and Recovery Strategies}

A BitTorrent client operates in an inherently unreliable environment. Peers come
and go, network connections fail, trackers become temporarily unavailable, and
data may be corrupted in transit. Robust error handling is essential for
completing downloads despite these challenges.

\subsubsection{Error Classification}

We classify errors into three categories based on severity and recoverability:

\begin{verbatim}
  Error Type        Example                    Recovery Strategy
  ----------        -------                    -----------------
  Transient         Network timeout            Retry with backoff
  Peer-specific     Invalid message            Disconnect peer, continue
  Fatal             Disk full                  Exit with error
\end{verbatim}

\textbf{Transient errors} are temporary failures that may succeed on retry.
Examples include tracker timeouts, connection refused (peer busy), and temporary
network issues. The strategy is exponential backoff: wait longer between retries
to avoid hammering a struggling service.

\textbf{Peer-specific errors} affect only one peer connection. Examples include
protocol violations (invalid message format), handshake failures (wrong info hash),
and peer disconnections. The strategy is to disconnect the problematic peer and
continue with others. The pool of available peers allows us to replace bad actors.

\textbf{Fatal errors} prevent the download from continuing. Examples include disk
full, output file permission denied, and corrupted metainfo. The strategy is to
log the error clearly and exit with a non-zero status.

\subsubsection{Graceful Degradation}

When non-fatal errors occur, the client degrades gracefully rather than failing:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{All peers disconnect}: Continue running; re-announce timer will
  fetch new peers. Log at WARN level so user knows progress is stalled.

\item \textbf{Tracker unreachable}: Continue with existing peers. Use exponential
  backoff (30s, 60s, 120s, 240s, 480s) for retries, capped at 15 minutes.

\item \textbf{Piece hash fails}: Discard the piece, mark as failed, re-download
  from a different peer if possible. Log the failure for debugging.

\item \textbf{Block request times out}: Cancel the request, reassign to another
  peer that has the piece. If no alternative, the scheduler will retry later.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Retry Logic}

Block requests use a timeout-and-reassign strategy:

\begin{verbatim}
  Request block from peer A
       |
       | 30 seconds pass, no response
       v
  Request timed out
       |
       | Send Cancel to peer A
       | Find peer B that has piece and is not choking us
       |
  +----+----+
  | Found   | Not found
  v         v
  Request   Leave for scheduler
  from B    to retry later
\end{verbatim}

Tracker re-announces use exponential backoff:

\begin{verbatim}
  Attempt re-announce
       |
  +----+----+
  | Success | Failure
  v         v
  Use       Increment failure count
  tracker-  Calculate backoff: 30s * 2^failures
  provided  Cap at 15 minutes (900s)
  interval  Schedule retry with backoff
\end{verbatim}

\subsubsection{Error Logging Philosophy}

We use structured logging (via [[tracing]]) with consistent severity levels:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{ERROR}: Fatal conditions that will cause exit.
\item \textbf{WARN}: Non-fatal problems affecting progress (all peers gone,
  tracker down, hash failures).
\item \textbf{INFO}: Normal operational events (peer connected, piece verified,
  progress updates).
\item \textbf{DEBUG}: Detailed protocol events (message sent/received, state
  changes).
\item \textbf{TRACE}: Very detailed internals (event loop iterations, timer
  ticks).
\end{itemize}

Each log message includes relevant context (peer address, piece index, error
details) as structured fields, making it easy to filter and analyze logs.

\subsection{Connection Manager Extension}

We need to add a method to [[PeerConnectionManager]] to access the internal
connections map for the event loop:

<<event loop connection manager extension>>=
impl PeerConnectionManager {
    /// Returns a reference to the internal connections map.
    ///
    /// This is used by the event loop to find alternative peers for
    /// reassigning block requests. The method provides read-only access
    /// for querying peer state without modifying connections.
    pub(crate) fn connections_map(&self) -> &HashMap<SocketAddr, PeerConnection> {
        &self.connections
    }

    /// Returns a mutable reference to the internal connections map.
    ///
    /// This is used by the event loop to read from multiple peer streams
    /// concurrently. The method is intentionally not public in the normal
    /// API since direct map access bypasses the manager's invariants.
    pub(crate) fn connections_map_mut(&mut self) -> &mut HashMap<SocketAddr, PeerConnection> {
        &mut self.connections
    }
}
